@page "/azure"
@rendermode WasmRenderModes.Interactive
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client
@using PoPunkouterSoftware.Shared
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject HttpClient Http
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject NotificationService NotificationService
@inject NavigationManager NavManager

<PageTitle>PoPunkouterSoftware — Azure status</PageTitle>

@* data-snap-pager: on mobile portrait ONLY (see the [data-snap-pager] block in
   modern-ui.css), this container becomes exactly one viewport tall and each `.app-pane`
   child fills it, so a phone visitor moves a whole screen at a time instead of free-
   scrolling a long column. Nothing is hidden or cut - the panes are a grouping of
   the same sections the desktop layout stacks, and the one body of content that genuinely
   cannot be compressed into a screen (advanced diagnostics) opts out with
   `.app-pane--tall` and scrolls under its own steam.

   The panes are real elements at every viewport, not a mobile-only wrapper: on desktop
   `.app-pane` is an ordinary grid section, which is also what gives the page its vertical
   rhythm. Keep each pane to ONE question - that is what makes the paging legible, and
   PortfolioUiTests.Azure_MobilePortrait_PanesFitTheViewport is what stops a new section
   being dropped into whichever pane happens to be nearest. *@
@* <main id="main-content">, not a bare <div>: the skip link in MainLayout targets
   `#main-content` on EVERY page, and this page did not have one — so "Skip to content"
   was a link to nothing here while working correctly on "/". It is also the page's only
   main landmark. `tabindex="-1"` makes it a programmatic focus target, matching
   Index.razor. *@
<main id="main-content" class="app-page azure-ops-page" data-snap-pager tabindex="-1">
@* The error banner sits ALONGSIDE the dashboard, never instead of it — the rule
   Index.razor already follows for the catalog. A refresh whose summary reload fails is a
   STALE dashboard, not an empty one: `_summary` is left populated on failure (see
   LoadSummaryAsync) so a 503 on the post-rescan reload no longer deletes the hero, all six
   panes and the advanced tree that were on screen a second earlier. Only a failure with
   nothing to fall back on renders the banner alone. *@
@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_loadError))
{
    <ErrorRetryBanner Title="@(_summary is null ? "Azure status is unavailable." : "Azure status could not be updated — showing the last known scan.")"
                      Message="@_loadError" OnRetry="LoadSummaryAsync" />
}

@if (_loading && _summary is null)
{
    <LoadingSkeleton Variant="summary" TileCount="3" AriaLabel="Loading Azure status" />
}
else if (_summary is not null)
{
    var summary = _summary;
    var ageLabel = RelativeTime.Format(summary.GeneratedAt);
    // Tone is derived from the counts, never from the narrative text: parsing generated prose
    // to choose a colour is how a green band ends up above a paragraph about an outage.
    var narrativeTone = summary.BrokenServices > 0 ? "bad"
        : summary.ActionableCount > 0 ? "warn"
        : "good";
    // The queue replaces the plain attention list in place once the full report is in.
    var showQueue = _advancedOpen && report is not null;

    @* One pane for "what is going on": headline, the plain-English lead paragraph under it,
       and the numbers. The paragraph used to be a separate glass card in a pane of its own
       ABOVE the hero, so the page opened by saying the same thing in two boxes — prose about
       the outage, then an h1 counting it — before a single number. *@
    <section class="app-pane azure-pane-status">
    @* data-fx-origin: where the GPU backdrop's recovery bloom starts (js/gpu-backdrop.js). *@
    <header class="azure-command-header @(summary.AttentionCount > 0 ? "has-attention" : "is-healthy")" data-glass data-fx-origin="health">
        <div class="azure-command-copy">
            <div class="app-kicker">Azure · @summary.SubscriptionName</div>
            @* No badge beside the h1 any more. It said "N actionable", which differed from the
               headline count only by the staleness flag — and staleness has its own badge in
               the meta row below. Never put a badge, chip or count INSIDE a heading element:
               it joins the accessible name that <FocusOnNavigate Selector="h1"> announces. *@
            <h1 class="azure-hero-title">
                @(summary.AttentionCount == 0 ? "Everything looks good" : $"{summary.AttentionCount} item(s) need attention")
            </h1>
            <AzureStatusNarrative Summary="@CurrentAiSummary" AttentionItems="@summary.AttentionItems" Tone="@narrativeTone" />
            @* Freshness is stated ONCE. It used to be "Updated 3d ago", a Current/Stale badge,
               and the age again inside two KPI labels ("Health as of 3d ago", "Unavailable
               (3d ago)"). A green "Current" badge said nothing, so the badge now appears only
               when it is a warning. *@
            <div class="azure-command-meta">
                <AppHint Focusable="true"
                         Text="@($"Health, cost and uptime on this page are from the last full Azure scan, {ageLabel}.{(summary.IsStale ? " That is past the freshness window, so treat them as a snapshot rather than as the state of the estate now." : "")}")">
                    Updated @ageLabel
                </AppHint>
                @if (summary.IsStale)
                {
                    <RadzenBadge Text="Stale data" IsPill="true" BadgeStyle="BadgeStyle.Danger" />
                }
            </div>
        </div>
        @* Three tiles. Health % and the unavailable count were two tiles for one ratio (the
           percentage IS healthy/total), so the count now rides on the health tile's label.
           The series are projections of the same stored history, and a series with fewer
           than two points renders nothing rather than a misleading single dot.
           role="group" is what makes the aria-label count: a bare <div> takes no name. *@
        <div class="app-kpis azure-command-kpis" role="group" aria-label="Azure status summary">
            <KpiTile Value="@($"{summary.HealthPercent}%")"
                     Label="@(summary.BrokenServices == 0 ? "Service health" : $"Health · {summary.BrokenServices} down")">
                <Sparkline Points="@summary.HealthHistory" Tone="good" AriaLabel="Service health over the last 30 scans" />
            </KpiTile>
            <KpiTile Value="@summary.CostFormatted" Label="30-day cost">
                <Sparkline Points="@summary.CostHistory" Tone="accent" AriaLabel="30-day cost over the last 30 scans" />
            </KpiTile>
            <KpiTile Value="@summary.TotalResources.ToString()" Label="Resources">
                <Sparkline Points="@summary.ResourceHistory" Tone="accent" AriaLabel="Resource count over the last 30 scans" />
            </KpiTile>
        </div>
        <div class="azure-command-actions">
            @if (_managementActionsEnabled)
            {
                @* Named for what it actually does. "Refresh" reads like the page reloads;
                   this starts a full ARM subscription scan that takes about half a minute,
                   which is worth knowing before clicking it. *@
                <RadzenButton Text="@(_refreshing ? "Rescanning Azure…" : "Rescan Azure")" Icon="refresh"
                              ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary" Size="ButtonSize.Small" Disabled="@_refreshing" Click="RefreshAsync"
                              data-fx-origin="refresh"
                              title="Runs a full Azure subscription scan (roughly 30 seconds). Progress streams live, and you can leave the page while it runs." />
                @if (_refreshing)
                {
                    <RadzenButton Text="Cancel" Icon="close" ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Danger" Size="ButtonSize.Small" Click="CancelRefreshAsync" />
                }
            }
            else
            {
                @* The config keys that would enable rescan are owner-facing, so they live in the
                   hint, not on the page every visitor reads. Focusable: it is the only place a
                   keyboard or phone visitor learns why there is no rescan button. *@
                <span class="app-note">Read-only <AppHint Focusable="true"
                    Text="Management actions are disabled on this deployment. Set FeatureFlags:EnableManagementActions=true (and Security:ManagementApiKey for the extra API-key gate) to enable rescan and cancel." /></span>
            }
            @* Sonification. A second MODALITY on facts this page already renders — not a
               second copy of them — so the "each fact appears once" rule is intact: nothing
               new is reported here, the same summary is played instead of read.

               Outside the management gate on purpose: it costs nothing, calls no endpoint
               and mutates nothing, so it belongs with /api/diag/ai and the snooze endpoints
               on the unprivileged side. Clicking it while sound is off turns sound on — the
               click is itself the user gesture the autoplay policy requires, so this is the
               one place where opting in and hearing the result can happen in one action. *@
            <RadzenButton Text="Listen" Icon="graphic_eq" ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Light" Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                          Click="SonifyAsync" Disabled="@_sonifying"
                          title="Plays the fleet status as a four-second sound: health tunes the chord, outages add dissonance, spend sets the pulse rate, uptime opens the filter. Turns sound on if it is off." />
        </div>
    </header>

    @if (_refreshing)
    {
        @* @ref, and Update() rather than parameters: a hub tick must not cost a page render.
           See AzureRefreshProgress.razor and the hub wiring in AzureDashboard.razor.cs. *@
        <AzureRefreshProgress @ref="_progressUi"
                              Percent="@_progressPercent" Step="@_progressStep"
                              Determinate="@(_hub?.State == HubConnectionState.Connected)" />
    }
    </section>

    @* One grid, three cards. There used to be two grids and four cards, and the extra card
       was a donut restating the health ratio the hero already gives as a percentage — the
       same fact appeared in the hero tile, the card subtitle and the donut centre. The cost
       card likewise drew `CostHistory` whenever it had more than one point, which is the
       exact series the hero's cost sparkline already draws; it now always shows the per-
       resource-group breakdown, which appears nowhere else. Trend in the sparkline,
       breakdown in the chart, ratio in the hero — each fact rendered once. *@
    <section class="app-pane azure-pane-spend">
    <section class="azure-glance-grid" aria-label="Spend forecast and Azure status charts">
        <AzureCostForecast Forecast="@summary.Forecast" />
        <article class="app-card" data-glass>
            <h2 class="azure-glance-title">30-day cost by resource group</h2>
            <div class="azure-glance-subtitle" aria-hidden="true">Top cost drivers this scan</div>
            @if (summary.CostDrivers.Count == 0)
            {
                <div class="azure-compact-state">No cost breakdown is available.</div>
            }
            else
            {
                @* MetricBars, not RadzenChart - at most five pre-sorted rows. See the header
                   of MetricBars.razor for why a chart was the wrong shape for a top-N. *@
                <MetricBars Points="@summary.CostDrivers" Tone="accent" Format="money" />
            }
            <span class="app-sr-only">@string.Join(", ", summary.CostDrivers.Select(x => $"{x.Label}: ${x.Value:F2}"))</span>
        </article>
        <article class="app-card" data-glass>
            <h2 class="azure-glance-title">Response time · slowest endpoints</h2>
            <div class="azure-glance-subtitle">
                <span aria-hidden="true">Current scan, milliseconds</span>
                <AppHint Focusable="true" Text="Free-tier apps sleep when idle — multi-second times are usually cold starts, not outages." />
            </div>
            @if (summary.ResponseTimes.Count == 0)
            {
                <div class="azure-compact-state success">No slow responses detected.</div>
            }
            else
            {
                <MetricBars Points="@summary.ResponseTimes" Tone="warm" Format="ms" />
            }
            <span class="app-sr-only">@string.Join(", ", summary.ResponseTimes.Select(x => $"{x.Label}: {x.Value:N0} milliseconds"))</span>
        </article>
    </section>

    </section>

    @* 30 day-columns at 10px: a phone scrolls them inside their own container, and a
       desktop half (~630px) holds the whole grid, so it pairs with "what changed" there. *@
    <section class="app-pane azure-pane-uptime">
    <AzureUptimeHeatmap Map="@summary.Uptime" Focus="@Focus" />
    </section>

    @* Its own pane: "what moved" is the question a returning visitor actually arrives with,
       and it deserves a screen rather than a footnote under the hero. Answered from stored
       history with no extra request. AFTER uptime, not straight after the hero: on desktop
       the two are a half-width pair (see the >=1100px block in the stylesheet), which frees
       the spend pane to span the page its three cards need. DOM order is the pairing order,
       so reading and tab order still match what is on screen. *@
    <section class="app-pane azure-pane-changed">
    <AzureWhatChanged Delta="@summary.Changes" />
    </section>

    @* ONE list of what needs doing. First paint shows the server's plain attention sentences;
       once Advanced diagnostics has loaded the full report, the same section shows the ranked
       queue (commands, impact, snooze) instead. They used to be two lists on one page — this
       one plus a "Full priority queue" further down — and the security/cleanup count badges
       that sat in this heading repeated two of the sentences directly beneath them.

       --tall while the queue is showing: it is as long as the estate is broken, and like the
       advanced pane it scrolls rather than being cut. *@
    <section class="app-pane azure-pane-actions @(showQueue ? "app-pane--tall" : null)">
    <section class="azure-attention-summary">
        <div class="azure-section-heading">
            <h2>Needs attention</h2>
            @* ButtonStyle.Base (not .Light): Radzen's "Light" style hardcodes `color:
               var(--rz-base-200)` for the outlined variant in every theme — a near-white label,
               invisible on the light-mode surface. Base resolves to --rz-text-color, which the
               token bridge maps to --app-text, so it tracks both schemes. *@
            <div class="azure-advanced-toggle">
                <RadzenButton Text="@(_advancedOpen ? "Hide advanced diagnostics" : "Advanced diagnostics")"
                              Icon="@(_advancedOpen ? "expand_less" : "tune")" ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Base"
                              Variant="Variant.Outlined" Size="ButtonSize.Small" Click="ToggleAdvancedAsync" />
            </div>
        </div>
        @if (showQueue)
        {
            <AzurePriorityQueue Items="@PriorityQueue"
                                BadgeFor="@ActionabilityBadge"
                                OnCopyCommand="@CopyText"
                                OnSnooze="@SnoozePriorityItemAsync" />
            <AzureSnoozedItems Items="@_activeSnoozes" OnUnsnooze="@UnsnoozeEntryAsync" />
        }
        else if (summary.AttentionItems.Count == 0)
        {
            <div class="azure-compact-state success"><RadzenIcon Icon="check_circle" /> No action is needed.</div>
        }
        else
        {
            <ol class="azure-simple-attention">
                @foreach (var item in summary.AttentionItems) { <li>@item</li> }
            </ol>
        }
    </section>
    </section>

    @if (_advancedOpen)
    {
        @* --tall: the only body of content on this page that cannot honestly be compressed
           into one screen. It still snaps to the top of the viewport, then scrolls
           internally - the alternative would be to truncate it, which is exactly what the
           paging exists to avoid. *@
        <section class="app-pane app-pane--tall azure-advanced" aria-label="Advanced Azure diagnostics">
        @* Banner alongside, not instead of — see the note on the page-level banner above. *@
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_advancedError))
        {
            <ErrorRetryBanner Title="@(report is null ? "Diagnostics could not be loaded." : "Diagnostics could not be updated — showing the last load.")"
                              Message="@_advancedError" OnRetry="LoadReportAsync" />
        }

        @if (_advancedLoading && report is null)
        {
            <RadzenProgressBar Mode="ProgressBarMode.Indeterminate" />
        }
        else if (report is not null)
        {
            var currentReport = report;
            var securityIssueCount = (currentReport.StorageInventory ?? new()).Sum(s => s.IssueCount)
                + (currentReport.ConfigDrift ?? new()).Sum(d => d.IssueCount);

            <AzureResourceExplorer Items="@FilteredResourceExplorerItems"
                                   TotalCount="@ResourceExplorerItems.Count"
                                   Views="@ResourceViews"
                                   SelectedView="@_resourceView"
                                   SelectedViewChanged="@SetResourceView"
                                   StatusBadgeFor="@ExplorerStatusBadge"
                                   RiskClassFor="@RiskClass">
                <HeadingActions>
                    @* One-tap: the primary action fires on the main button; the secondary export
                       lives behind the same control's caret instead of a second sibling button.
                       It sits in the explorer's heading row rather than a toolbar row of its own. *@
                    <RadzenSplitButton Text="Download report" Icon="download" Size="ButtonSize.Small" Click="OnDownloadClick">
                        <RadzenSplitButtonItem Text="Full report (JSON)" Value="json" Icon="data_object" />
                        <RadzenSplitButtonItem Text="Audit script (PowerShell)" Value="script" Icon="terminal" />
                    </RadzenSplitButton>
                </HeadingActions>
            </AzureResourceExplorer>

            <AzureEvidenceDisclosures Report="@currentReport"
                                      SecurityIssueCount="@securityIssueCount"
                                      StepTimingTotalMs="@StepTimingTotalMs" />
        }
        </section>
    }
}
</main>
